>
  <h2>The Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Electromagnetic field exposure from routers, smartphones, smart meters, and the
    proliferating Wi-Fi-connected devices in modern homes represents a chronic, low-grade
    biological stressor with no historical parallel. Human biology did not evolve in a
    constant-RF environment. The research on non-thermal EMF effects — particularly on
    sleep quality, mitochondrial function, and oxidative stress — is not conclusive,
    but it is not empty either.
  </p>
  <p>
    The precautionary principle applies here with minimal downside: the interventions
    that reduce EMF exposure (distance from devices, wired connections, turning off
    routers at night) have zero cost and negligible inconvenience. If there is a
    measurable biological effect, you've eliminated it. If there isn't, you've lost
    nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The bedroom is the critical target. You spend 7–8 hours per night in that room at
    close range to whatever devices live there. A router on the nightstand or a phone
    charging six inches from your head is the highest practical exposure scenario — and
    the easiest to fix.
  </p>

  <h2>The Insulting Behaviors</h2>
  <ul class="insult-list">
    <li>Smartphone in your front pocket all day — proximity maximizes exposure across the most sensitive tissue</li>
    <li>Router in or adjacent to the bedroom — broadcasting full-power RF 24 hours a day</li>
    <li>Sleeping with your phone charging on the nightstand — 6–8 hours of close-range exposure during recovery</li>
    <li>Smart meters on a shared wall with the bedroom — utility-grade transmitters updating every 15–30 minutes</li>
    <li>Wi-Fi-connected devices throughout every room — cumulative ambient RF load with no off-state</li>
    <li>Never taking a device-free period — no window of reduced EMF load in the entire day</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="section-eliminate">Eliminate the Insulting Behavior</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 1 — Phone Out of the Bedroom</h3>
      <p>Move your phone charger to another room. Use a traditional alarm clock. This is free, immediate, and eliminates your highest-duration close-proximity exposure.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 2 — Router on a Timer</h3>
      <p>Plug the router into an outlet timer set to cut power during sleep hours. Eliminates all Wi-Fi RF exposure for 7–8 hours per night at zero cost.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 3 — Hardwire Where Possible</h3>
      <p>Ethernet connections for desktop computers, TVs, and stationary devices eliminate those Wi-Fi nodes from your ambient environment entirely.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Step 4 — Distance and Shielding for High-Exposure Areas</h3>
      <p>For proximity to smart meters or high-density RF environments, EMF-shielding bedding and faraday phone cases provide measurable attenuation.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

